course: medicine
lesson: 4
title: Cuts, grazes, stitches and splinters
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Your skin is a wall that keeps germs out, and a cut or graze is a hole in that wall.
explain: When you graze your knee in the playground, clean running water washing out the grit does the main job; stinging antiseptic is not needed for most grazes.
explain: A cut that bleeds a lot needs firm pressure with a clean cloth for about 10 minutes without peeping, so the blood can clot and plug the hole.
explain: Deep or gaping cuts heal better when the edges are held together by stitches, medical glue or paper strips.
explain: The scab is the body's own plaster, so leave it alone; red, hot, puffy skin with pus means germs got in and someone should look at it.
fact: A graze scrapes off the top layer of skin, and dirt left in it can cause an infection.
fact: Rinsing a graze under clean running water washes out grit; stinging antiseptic is not needed for most grazes.
fact: Pressing firmly on a bleeding cut for about 10 minutes and raising it helps a clot form; lifting the cloth to look starts the bleeding again.
fact: Deep or gaping cuts are closed with stitches, medical skin glue or paper strips, after the area is numbed; edges held together heal better and leave a smaller scar.
fact: Some stitches dissolve by themselves; others come out after about a week, which feels like a tug.
fact: Medical skin glue falls off by itself after one or two weeks; superglue from home must not be used on cuts.
fact: A splinter is pulled out with clean tweezers at the same angle it went in; digging with a needle can push it deeper, and a deep one, for example under a nail, may need a nurse.
fact: Tetanus germs live in soil and can get into deep or dirty wounds; the danger is the dirt, not the rust, and a tetanus jab protects you, so nurses ask whether you have had one.
fact: Redness, heat, swelling and yellow pus are signs of infection; pus is dead white blood cells and germs.
fact: A spreading red area, fever or red lines up the arm need a doctor quickly.
fact: A scab is a dried blood clot protecting the new skin; picking it reopens the wound and can leave a bigger scar; an itchy scab is a normal part of healing.
fact: Some people faint at the sight of blood because their heart slows and blood pressure drops; it is a reflex, not weakness.
fact: Paper cuts hurt a lot because fingertips have huge numbers of nerve endings and the cut is ragged and shallow.
exercise: What is the main way to clean a gritty graze? | Rinse it with clean running water | Pour on lots of stinging antiseptic until it fizzes | Rub a little soap and mud into it | Leave the grit in to protect it from germs
exercise: How do you stop a cut from bleeding a lot? | Press firmly with a clean cloth for about 10 minutes | Lift the cloth every few seconds to check if it stopped | Run it under hot water for a minute | Let it bleed until it stops all by itself
exercise: Why are deep cuts stitched? | Edges held together heal better with a smaller scar | Stitches make the cut stop hurting for ever | All deep cuts must be stitched by law | Stitches stop any germs from living anywhere on your body
exercise: What does having stitches taken out usually feel like? | A small tug | A big hot burn | A deep sharp stab | Nothing at all, ever
exercise: Why did the nurse use glue instead of stitches? | Medical glue holds a small cut without needles | Glue makes any cut heal completely in an hour | She had simply run out of thread | Glue means the cut will never leave a scar
exercise: Can you fix a cut at home with superglue from the drawer? | No, only medical glue is made and tested for skin | Yes, any glue from the drawer works just the same | Yes, as long as you use lots of it | No, put sticky tape inside the cut instead
exercise: How should a grown-up take out a splinter? | With clean tweezers at the angle it went in | By digging around under the skin with a pin | By squeezing the finger as hard as possible | By pushing it right through and out the other side
exercise: Why did the nurse ask about your tetanus jab? | Tetanus germs from soil can get into dirty wounds | Tetanus only ever comes from rusty nails | The jab makes cuts heal faster and stops scars | Nurses ask everyone that just to fill in time
exercise: Your cut is red, hot, puffy and has yellow stuff in it. What's going on? | It may be infected and should be shown to a nurse or doctor | It is healing perfectly and the yellow stuff is new skin | It is just extra skin growing too fast | The scab has melted in the warm weather
exercise: What is pus? | Dead white blood cells and germs | Fresh new skin that is still soft | Water soaked out of the plaster | Melted fat from under the skin
exercise: Why shouldn't you pick your scab? | It reopens the wound and can leave a bigger scar | Scabs are poisonous to touch with bare fingers | It makes the cut heal faster but hurts | The scab will grow back twice as big
exercise: Your friend fainted when he saw your cut. What should be done? | Lay him down with his legs raised | Splash him with cold water and stand him straight up | Give him a sweet while he stays standing | Shake him hard by the shoulders
exercise: Why does a tiny paper cut hurt so much? | Fingertips have loads of nerve endings | Paper has a poison in it that stings | The cut is secretly very deep | Paper cuts always get infected straight away
exercise: Which needs a doctor quickly? | A red area spreading up the arm with fever | A small itchy scab on the knee that is drying | A tiny paper cut on one finger | A graze that has been rinsed and covered
exercise: Why raise a bleeding hand? | It helps slow the bleeding while you press | It makes the blood flow out faster and clean it | It dries the cut out in the air | It stops you seeing the blood and fainting
exercise: What protects new skin while a cut heals? | The scab, a dried blood clot | Dirt that sticks to the cut and hardens | Air bubbles trapped under the skin | Hair growing over the top of the cut
test: blood everywhere, I keep lifting the tissue to check if it stopped. fine? | No, keep pressing without peeping for about 10 minutes | Yes, checking loads helps it stop quicker | Yes, lift it every second to let air in | No, wipe it with a dry leaf from outside
test: Josh says it's only rusty nails that give you tetanus. Right? | No, the danger is germs from soil in dirty wounds | Yes, only rust is the problem, clean nails are fine | Yes, and only the orange rust | No, tetanus comes from bee stings mostly
test: Your scab is so itchy! Is the itch a bad sign? | No, itching is part of normal healing | Yes, it means a bad infection is starting | Yes, it means the cut is getting deeper underneath | No, it means the scab is ready to pick off
test: Mum wants to pour stinging antiseptic on my little graze. What's the advice? | Clean running water usually does the main job | Antiseptic must always sting or it isn't working | Grazes need a splash of vinegar | Grazes should never get wet while they heal
test: In a cartoon, a hero sticks his cut together with tape from the shed and carries on. What's wrong? | Deep cuts need proper medical care to close and stay clean | Nothing, tape from the shed is better than stitches | Nothing, cuts never need any care | He should have used superglue from the shed
test: My splinter's deep under the nail. Can I dig it out with Mum's sewing needle? | Better not; a nurse may need to remove it | Yes, dig as deep as you like till it's out | Yes, but heat the needle red hot first to kill germs | No, just leave it in there for a year
test: What would happen if a deep dirty wound were never cleaned? | Germs could cause an infection | The dirt would slowly turn into new skin | It would heal faster than a clean one | Nothing, a bit of dirt is good for wounds
test: Ella fainted when she saw blood, and Tom called her a wimp. Who is wrong? | Tom; fainting at blood is a common reflex | Ella; she was faking it to get out of class | Neither, fainting means you are weak | Tom, because only doctors are allowed to faint
test: Which of these shows a cut is getting infected? | It gets redder, hotter and puffier with pus | It forms a dry brown scab on top | It itches a little at night | It slowly turns pink and smooth over a week
test: The red line is creeping up my arm from my cut and I feel hot. Wait a week? | No, see a doctor quickly | Yes, it will pass on its own | Yes, red lines are a healthy sign | No, just pick the scab off to let it out
test: Why do they numb the skin before stitches? | So putting in stitches does not hurt much | To make the cut close up by itself | To kill all the germs inside the cut | So you fall asleep while they sew
exam: Identify the principal measure for the arrest of bleeding from a cut. | Firm and sustained pressure applied with a clean cloth | Repeated removal of the dressing to inspect the wound | Application of hot water to the wound for some minutes | Unimpeded bleeding until spontaneous cessation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the risk of tetanus? | It arises from soil-borne organisms entering deep or contaminated wounds | It arises exclusively from contact with rust upon old metal | It arises from the sight of blood | It arises from the removal of stitches before the wound has closed
exam: It is given that a wound has become red, hot and swollen and is discharging pus. State the most probable explanation. | The wound has become infected | The wound is healing in the ordinary manner | The scab has been reabsorbed into the skin | Excess skin is being formed at the margins
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the cleansing of a superficial graze. | Irrigation with clean running water is the principal measure | Antiseptic causing stinging is indispensable to cleansing | The wound is to be kept entirely dry and unwashed | Grit is to be left in situ as protection against infection
